TK=[
 ('Time compensation:', 'Advancing and delaying the entraining light cycle rotate migratory headings in opposite directions. A compass bearing is distinct from a geographic map or a complete migration route.'),
 ('Antennal clocks:', 'Either light-accessible antenna can support compensation. Conflicting antennal timing disrupts the shared bearing, and removing the conflicting antenna restores orientation.'),
 ('Molecular feedback:', 'CRY1 supplies light-sensitive clock input, while CRY2 represses clock-controlled transcription. Protein interactions and timed nuclear localization contribute to the feedback cycle.'),
 ('Converging visual cues:', 'Central-complex neurons encode polarization and sun-like azimuth. Neural sensitivity to polarization does not establish its necessity for migration when the sun is available.'),
 ('Seasonal reversal:', 'Effective multiweek cold exposure induces northward orientation while time compensation remains available. Warm aging alone did not produce reversal in the tested migrants.'),
 ('Representations and limits:', 'Current heading and desired direction have distinct neuronal correlates. A complete causal pathway from antennal timing through compass computation to steering remains unidentified.')]
